Nature Climate Change
                             (2026) Cite this article










                    Save article





                View saved research

AbstractExtreme southward swings of the South Indian convergence zone can induce devastating floods over parts of southern Africa. How greenhouse warming affects such southward swings is not well understood. Here we show that, under a high-emissions scenario, the frequency of extreme southward swings doubles from one event per 22 years in the twentieth century to one per 10 years in the twenty-first century with an average increase in rainfall intensity of 20%. The increased frequency is related to faster warming of the African continent than of the Indian Ocean that intensifies northerly winds, and to a poleward migration of Southern Ocean westerlies that drives a pattern of faster subtropical Indian Ocean warming to the south and enhances Atlantic-to-Africa moisture transport, both of which favour a southward swing of the South Indian convergence zone. More frequent southward migration of the South Indian convergence zone might thus indicate increasing flooding risk in parts of southern Africa.

