          Additional informationPublisher’s note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations.Extended dataExtended Data Fig. 1 Observed SICZ index and extreme southward swings.a, Time series of observed SICZ index obtained from an EOF analysis on DJF precipitation anomalies over southern Africa (20° E–45° E, 30° S–5° S) during the 1960–2024 period (‘Depiction of SICZ meridional swings’ in Methods). b, Relationship between the SICZ index and SICZ position anomalies defined by the latitude of DJF rainfall maximum. Correlation coefficient r and corresponding P value are also shown. Red dots denote four extreme southward swings of the SICZ, while blue dots indicate seven moderate events. c–f, DJF rainfall (mm day−1, colour shading) and 850 hPa wind anomalies (m s−1, vectors) during observed extreme southward swing events in c 1998/1999, d 1999/2000, e 2016/2017 and f 2020/2021. The SICZ index based on rainfall reflects SICZ meridional swings, and an SICZ extreme southward swing brings an anomalously wet condition over southern Africa. Basemap data in c–f from Natural Earth (https://www.naturalearthdata.com).Extended Data Fig. 2 Moisture budget associated with SICZ meridional swings.a-c, Observed regression of moisture budget terms (mm d–1, colour shading) onto the SICZ index during the 1960–2024 period (‘Atmospheric moisture budget’ in Methods), including a zonal dynamic term (DYNZ), b meridional dynamic term (DYNM) and c thermodynamic term (TD). d, Same as a-c, but for vertically integrated specific humidity anomalies (Kg m−2). The transient eddy term is negligible (not shown). Stippling indicates statistical significance above the 95% level based on a two-tailed Student’s t-test. The SICZ swings are predominantly controlled by mean moisture advection by anomalous zonal and meridional winds. Basemap data from Natural Earth (https://www.naturalearthdata.com).Extended Data Fig. 3 Model assessment in simulating the SICZ.a, Squared correlation coefficient (R2) between modelled SICZ index and latitudinal position anomalies of the SICZ in each of 43 CMIP6 models (x-axis) and latitude-weighted spatial RMSE (mm d−1) between DJF mean rainfall in (30°S–5°S, 20°E–45°E) in each model and observation (y-axis). The vertical dashed line indicates half of the observed value and the horizontal dashed line indicates 2.5 mm d−1. The 20 models in the lower-right red box are selected. b, c, MME DJF mean rainfall in (b) 20 selected models and (c) 23 non-selected models. d, e, Relationship between the SICZ index and latitudinal position anomalies of the SICZ for (d) selected, and (e) non-selected models. Linear fits (grey line) are shown, together with squared correlation coefficient R2. Selecting models based on the simulated mean rainfall and the relationship between our SICZ index and latitude of rainfall maximum yields a group of realistic models that reasonably simulate the mean state and meridional swings of the SICZ. Basemap data in b and c from Natural Earth (https://www.naturalearthdata.com).Extended Data Fig. 4 Sensitivity to model selection and emission scenarios.a, Comparison of SICZ extreme southward swing frequency (events per 100 years) between 1900–1999 (blue bars) and 2000–2099 (red bars) in all models under SSP5-8.5 and their MME. Models that simulate a decrease are greyed out. Error bar on the MME is calculated as 1.0 s.d. of 10,000 inter-realizations of a bootstrap method. b-d, Comparison of multi-model histogram of SICZ index during moderate and extreme southward swings (SICZ index>1.0) over 1900–1999 (blue bars) and 2000–2099 (red bars) under SSP1-2.6 (17 models), SSP2-4.5 (18 models) and SSP3-7.0 (15 models). Increased frequency of SICZ southward swings under greenhouse warming does not depend on model selection or emission scenarios.Extended Data Fig. 5 Little impact of ENSO change on increase in southward swings of the SICZ.a, Inter-model relationship between changes in the number of southward swings (SICZ index>1.0 s.d.) and change in ENSO variability (s.d.) between 2000–2099 and 1900–1999, both scaled by the increase in global mean SST of each model between the two periods (per °C of global warming). b, As in a, but for inter-model relationship between changes in the number of southward swings and of extreme La Niña events. Here, ENSO variability is measured by standard deviation (s.d.) of DJF Niño3.4 index (area-weighted average SST anomalies in 170° W–120° W, 5° S–5° N), and an extreme La Niña is defined as when the DJF Niño3.4 index less than −1.5 s.d. Linear fits (solid black line) are shown, together with correlation coefficient r and corresponding P value. Increased ENSO variability under greenhouse warming is not a cause for the increased frequency of SICZ southward swings.Extended Data Fig. 6 Northward movement of the mean-state SICZ accompanied by an increased frequency of anomalous SICZ extreme southward swings.a, Multi-model mean change in DJF mean rainfall (mm d–1 century–1, colour shading) between 2000–2099 and 1900–1999. Hatching indicates statistical significance above the 95% level based on a two-tailed Student’s t-test. Overlaid are contours of multi-model mean 4, 6 and 8 mm d–1 of DJF total rainfall during 1900–1999 (blue lines). b, Comparison of multi-model histogram of SICZ index using non-detrended DJF rainfall anomalies during extreme southward swings over 1900–1999 (blue bars) and 2000–2099 (red bars). The numbers of extreme southward swings are also indicated for each period. c, Comparison of SICZ extreme southward swing frequency (when the SICZ index defined using non-detrended DJF rainfall anomalies is larger than 1.5 s.d., in unit of events per 100 years) between 1900–1999 (blue bars) and 2000–2099 (red bars) in the 20 selected models under SSP5-8.5 and their MME. Models that simulate a decrease are greyed out. Error bar is calculated as 1.0 s.d. of 10,000 inter-realizations of a bootstrap method. Despite a northward movement of the mean SICZ, frequency of SICZ extreme southward swings increases even without removing the trend in the mean. Basemap data in a from Natural Earth (https://www.naturalearthdata.com).Extended Data Fig. 7 Conducive background mean-state changes.a, Multi-model mean changes of grid-point surface temperature (colour shading) between 1900–1999 and 2000–2099, scaled by the increase in global mean SST in each of the 20 selected models before averaging. b, As in a, but for mean-state changes in SLP (green contours; positive in solid lines and negative in dashed lines, with an interval of 8 Pa °C−1) and 850hPa winds (vectors). Boxes indicate key regions of mean state changes that are conducive to the increased SICZ southward swings, including regions for the meridional winds (white box, 30°E–50°E, 23°S–15°S) and for SST (green box, 30°E–55°E, 34°S–24°S; blue box, 77°E–105°E, 30°S–20°S). Mean state changes feature a strengthening southward low-level wind induced by an enhanced ocean-land warming contrast, and a positive SIOD-like SST warming pattern underpinned by a southward migration of the Southern Hemisphere midlatitude westerlies. Basemap data from Natural Earth (https://www.naturalearthdata.com).Extended Data Fig. 8 Spatial contrast in changes in the mean meridional winds favouring a southward swing of the SICZ.a, Changes in zonally averaged meridional wind changes at 850hPa (m s−1 per °C of global warming) over the SICZ region (20° E–45° E, 25° S–0° S). Solid black line and grey shading are multi-model mean and 1.0 s.d. of inter-model spread, respectively. Also shown are the multi-model distribution of mean SICZ position during 1900−1999 (blue) and 2000−2099 (red) in the 20 selected models. Box indicates the range between the 25th and the 75th percentile, whisker indicates the range between the 5th and the 95th percentile, and the multi-model ensemble median (white) are also shown. b, Comparison of multi-model histogram of SICZ indices from models, between 1900–1999 (blue bars) and 2000–2099 (red bars). Changes in SICZ meridional swings and mean state are both spatially non-uniform, such that increased occurrences of the SICZ southward swings and enhanced northerlies are both concentrated south of the mean SICZ location.Extended Data Fig. 9 Enhanced atmospheric flows from south-eastern Atlantic promote more SICZ southward swings.a, The first principal pattern (vectors, m s−1 °C−1 of global warming) of an inter-model multivariate EOF analysis on DJF 850hPa zonal and meridional wind changes between 1900–1999 and 2000–2099 in the south-eastern Atlantic and south-western Africa (40° S–0°, 10° W–30° E) across the 20 selected models. The corresponding PC is named PCwind(SouthAtlantic). b, Inter-model relationship between PCwind and PCwind(SouthAtlantic). c, Inter-model relationship between PCwind(SouthAtlantic) and changes in frequency of southward swings of the SICZ (number of events per °C of global warming). Linear fits (solid black line) are shown, together with correlation coefficient r and corresponding P value. The poleward migration of the westerlies induces a cyclonic circulation anomaly over the southeastern Atlantic, enhancing moisture transport from the Atlantic to southern Africa conducive to the increased frequency of SICZ southward swings. Basemap data in a from Natural Earth (https://www.naturalearthdata.com).Extended Data Fig. 10 Weak impact from increased variability of the SIOD.a, Multi-model mean regression pattern of SST anomalies onto the SIOD index, obtained as the first EOF of DJF SST anomalies over the south IO domain (30°E–120°E, 50°S–10°S) in each model. Hatching indicates statistical significance above the 95% level based on a two-tailed Student’s t-test. b, Inter-model relationship between changes in variability of the SIOD (s.d.) and in the number of southward swings (SICZ index>1.0 s.d.) of the SICZ between 2000–2099 and 1900–1999, both scaled by the increase in global mean SST of each model between the two periods (per °C of global warming). SIOD variability is measured by standard deviation (s.d.) of the normalised SIOD index between 1900–1999 and 2000–2099, respectively. A linear fit (solid black line) is shown, together with correlation coefficient r and corresponding P value. Changes in variability of the SIOD are not systematically responsible for the increased frequency of SICZ southward swings under greenhouse warming. Basemap data in a from Natural Earth (https://www.naturalearthdata.com).Supplementary informationSupplementary Information (download PDF )Supplementary Table 1 and Figs. 1–6.Peer Review File (download PDF )Rights and permissionsSpringer Nature or its licensor (e.g. a society or other partner) holds exclusive rights to this article under a publishing agreement with the author(s) or other rightsholder(s); author self-archiving of the accepted manuscript version of this article is solely governed by the terms of such publishing agreement and applicable law.Reprints and permissionsAbout this articleCite this articleWang, H., Geng, T., Cai, W. et al. Increased southward swings of the South Indian convergence zone under greenhouse warming.
